From d40d401233bcda07f470818614c93ce541f79c75 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Emaki contributors <emaki@localhost>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 21:00:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] services/polkit: run one request at a time and harden the
 flow

The service activated a request by taking it off the queue, so a second
request that arrived while a flow was active found a queue of one and
replaced the active flow. Requests now wait while a flow is active. A
finished flow is cleared before the next request starts, so
PolkitAgent.flow passes through null and is never replaced in place.

- The authentication cookie is no longer logged. The polkit library prints
  it when POLKIT_DEBUG is set, whatever the value; while a session
  starts, a print handler drops every message that contains it.
- A request with only unsupported identities is refused and the queue
  moves on instead of stalling.
- A session that completes while it is being initiated (no helper, no
  such user) cancels the request instead of starting another session
  recursively.
- Each request returns exactly one result to polkitd, holds its own
  reference to the cancellable, disconnects its handler and releases its
  GTask. A request that is already cancelled on arrival is refused.
- Selecting another identity is no longer counted as a failed attempt,
  and only identities offered for the request can be selected.
- Session::respond encodes the reply into a buffer it owns and clears it
  with explicit_bzero after polkit wrote it to the helper; the
  QString::toUtf8 copy was freed without being cleared. The QString and
  any QML copies of the reply cannot be cleared from here.
- AuthFlow gains details, subjectPid and callerPid (polkit.subject-pid
  and polkit.caller-pid). They describe the request; they are not an
  authenticated identity of the program that asked.
- PolkitAgent.cancelActiveRequest() cancels the active request, for
  example when the session locks: the caller is told it is not
  authorized and the queue moves on.

src/services/polkit/test drives the listener entry point with a fake
session, without the PolKit helper or PAM. A second test runs the
production Session against the polkit library with POLKIT_DEBUG set, using
an identity polkit refuses before it looks for a helper, and catches the
reply before it would reach one.
